  "slug": "integration-database-1fdf",
  "summary": "Recent comparative tools address pangenome compression, discordant HERV annotation across resources, and assembly-to-assembly comparison.",
  "synthesis": "Three recent publications address the problem of comparing genomic data across multiple representations. PanMAN offers a lossless mutation-annotated network format for pangenomes, reporting 3.5-1,391x compression over variation-preserving formats while encoding evolutionary relationships. A comparative analysis of DFAM, HERVd, and RepBase human endogenous retrovirus annotations finds major discrepancies in counts and coverage, with up to 93% of records reconciling across resources. ChromoMapper parses QUAST and alignment outputs to visualize collinearity, inconsistencies, and repeats between compared genome assemblies. The shared thread is comparison across multiple annotations or assemblies rather than a single reference. What is new is the explicit encoding of mutations within networks, quantified compression gains, and measured reconciliation of repeat annotations across databases.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-13"
}
